  <h2 class="kd-h2" id="games">Play a history game</h2>
  <p class="kd-sub">Games with real pictures and real dates, and a new crossword and quiz every day. The games start on Easy, and you can make them harder whenever you like.</p>
  <ul class="kd-games">
    <li><a class="kd-game" href="/games#pairs"><span class="kd-game-ic" aria-hidden="true">&#10022;</span><b>Emblem Pairs</b><span>Turn the cards and match the flags and coats of arms.</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="kd-game" href="/games#order"><span class="kd-game-ic" aria-hidden="true">&#8645;</span><b>Order the Ages</b><span>Put famous moments in the right order.</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="kd-game" href="/games#ranks"><span class="kd-game-ic" aria-hidden="true">&#9650;</span><b>Order the Ranks</b><span>Who was above whom? Put the ranks in order.</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="kd-game" href="/games#hunt"><span class="kd-game-ic" aria-hidden="true">&#9673;</span><b>Globe Hunt</b><span>Spin the globe and find where it was.</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="kd-game" href="/games#first"><span class="kd-game-ic" aria-hidden="true">&#9878;</span><b>Which Came First?</b><span>Two pictures: tap the older one.</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="kd-game" href="/games#puzzle"><span class="kd-game-ic" aria-hidden="true">&#10070;</span><b>Picture Puzzle</b><span>Swap the pieces to finish a famous picture.</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="kd-game" href="/games#words"><span class="kd-game-ic" aria-hidden="true">&#9998;</span><b>Word Hunt</b><span>Find hidden words about pharaohs, knights and more.</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="kd-game" href="/games#year"><span class="kd-game-ic" aria-hidden="true">&#10710;</span><b>Guess the Year</b><span>How old is it? Slide to your guess.</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="kd-game" href="/games#cross"><span class="kd-game-ic" aria-hidden="true">&#9636;</span><b>History Crossword</b><span>Fill in a new crossword of history every day.</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="kd-game" href="/write#ancient-egyptian"><span class="kd-game-ic" aria-hidden="true">&#9997;</span><b>Write Like a Scribe</b><span>Write your name in hieroglyphs, runes or cuneiform.</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="kd-game" href="/quiz"><span class="kd-game-ic" aria-hidden="true">?</span><b>Daily Quiz</b><span>A new history mystery to solve every day.</span></a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="kd-h2" id="who-was-who">Who was who?</h2>
  <p class="kd-sub">In many places long ago, the family you were born into decided a lot about your life: the work you did, who you could marry, even whether you were free. Climb down these ladders from the ruler at the top to the people at the bottom, then <a href="/societies/#born">be born into history</a> and see where chance puts you.</p>
  <div class="kc-ladders">
  <!-- build:kids-ladders -->
<article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Ancient Egypt</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hatshepsut.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Nesu (The pharaoh)</b> The pharaoh was Egypt’s ruler, who led the army, made the laws and was honoured almost like a god.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-egypt-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Seru (High officials)</b> Officials such as the vizier ran Egypt for the pharaoh, keeping its records and judging its court cases.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/herihor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Hem-netjer (Priests)</b> Priests looked after the temples, where they washed and dressed the gods’ statues and gave them food every day.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/egyptian-hieroglyphs.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Sesh (Scribes)</b> Scribes kept Egypt’s records, writing down harvests, taxes and court cases on rolls of papyrus.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/battle-of-kadesh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Mesha (Soldiers)</b> Egyptian soldiers fought on foot or from fast, two-wheeled chariots pulled by horses.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-egypt-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Hemu (Artisans)</b> Skilled craftsmen built and painted the royal tombs, and they went on strike when their food did not arrive.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-egypt-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Ihwty (Farmers)</b> Most Egyptians were farmers who grew wheat, barley and flax in fields watered by the Nile’s yearly flood.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-egypt-8.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Bak (Enslaved people)</b> Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; many were prisoners taken in war.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Ptolemaic Egypt</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ptolemy-i-soter.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Basileus (The king)</b> The king was a Greek from Macedonia who ruled Egypt as its pharaoh from the great city of Alexandria.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ptolemaic-egypt-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>The court and royal officials</b> Greek officials ran Egypt for the king, and they collected taxes from every province and village.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/rosetta-stone.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Egyptian priests</b> Egyptian priests looked after the temples, and the Greek kings built new temples to keep the priests on their side.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ptolemaic-egypt-5.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Machimoi (Egyptian soldiers)</b> Egyptian soldiers were given small farms too, and they helped the king’s army win a great battle at Raphia.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/faiyum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Peasant farmers</b> Most Egyptians were farmers who grew wheat beside the Nile and gave much of their harvest in taxes.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ptolemaic-egypt-8.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Enslaved people</b> Some people were enslaved, many of them brought from Syria and sold, and some tried to run away.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/aksum">the Kingdom of Aksum</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ezana.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Nəguś (The king of kings)</b> King Ezana of Aksum put his picture on gold coins and became one of the first kings anywhere to take up Christianity.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/aksum-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Royal kin, nobles and lesser kings</b> Aksum’s nobles were buried under tall carved stones that showed how important they were.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frumentius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Priests and the first clergy</b> Aksum’s first bishop, Frumentius, had once been a slave. He taught the young king and helped him become a Christian.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aksumite-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Merchants and foreign traders</b> Merchants at Aksum’s port of Adulis traded ivory and gold for silk and spices from faraway lands.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/aksum-5.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Farmers and herders</b> Most people in Aksum were farmers who grew a grain called teff and kept cattle, sheep and goats.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/aksum-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Enslaved people</b> Some people in Aksum were enslaved and sold to traders from far away across the sea.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/athens">Classical Athens</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pericles.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Pentakosiomedimnoi (Richest citizens)</b> The richest Athenians were expected to pay for warships and for the plays at the city’s festivals.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/athens-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Hippeis (Knights)</b> Knights were rich Athenians who could afford a horse and rode in the city’s cavalry.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/socrates.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Zeugitai (Hoplite class)</b> Farmers of the hoplite class bought their own shields and spears and fought side by side.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/athens-4.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Thetes (Poorer citizens)</b> Poorer citizen men could vote in the Assembly, serve on juries and row the city’s warships.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aristotle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Metoikoi (Metics)</b> Metics were free people from other places who lived and worked in Athens but could not vote.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/athens-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Douloi (Enslaved people)</b> Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; some were later freed.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/gaul">Gaul</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vercingetorix.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Vergobret (Kings and chief magistrates)</b> Each Gallic tribe had a council of elders and a leader, at first a king and later often a leader chosen every year.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/gaul-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Druides (Druids)</b> Druids were priests and teachers who learned everything by heart, settled quarrels and led the ceremonies for the gods.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/gaul-4.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Smiths and craftsmen</b> Gallic smiths made beautiful things from gold and bronze, decorated with swirling patterns.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/gaul-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Enslaved people</b> Some people in Gaul were enslaved, often after being captured in war, and their children were enslaved too.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/ancient-rome">Ancient Rome</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/trajan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Princeps (The emperor)</b> The emperor commanded the army, made the laws and had his face on the coins.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/roman-senate.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Ordo senatorius (Senators)</b> Senators were rich landowners who advised the emperor and governed the provinces.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pliny-the-elder.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Equites (Equestrians)</b> Equestrians, or knights, were wealthy men who ran the emperor’s offices, money and some of his armies.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-rome-4.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Decuriones (Town councillors)</b> Every Roman town had a council of rich local men who ran its markets, temples and festivals.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/auxilia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Milites (Soldiers)</b> Roman soldiers served for about 25 years and were given land or money when they retired.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/latifundium.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Plebs (Free people)</b> Most Romans were farmers, craftspeople and shopkeepers who worked hard to feed their families.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-rome-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Liberti (Freed slaves)</b> Enslaved people who were set free became Roman citizens, and their children were born free.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-rome-8.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Servi (Enslaved people)</b> Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; some were later set free.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/vikings">the Vikings</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/harald-bluetooth.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Konungar (Kings)</b> Viking kings led armies and fleets, gave gifts to their warriors and in time made their people Christian.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Jarlar (Jarls)</b> Jarls were powerful lords, one step below the king, who owned great farms and ruled whole regions.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-3.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Hersar (Chieftains)</b> Chieftains led the farmers of their district in war and at the assemblies where laws were made.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/varangian-guard.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Húskarlar (Retainers)</b> Kings and chieftains kept bands of warriors who lived with them and were rewarded with gifts.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-5.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Bœndr (Free farmers)</b> Most Vikings were farmers, not raiders: they grew grain, kept animals and met at assemblies to make laws.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Leysingjar (Freed slaves)</b> Some thralls were set free, and some were even given land to farm by their former owners.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/vikings-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Þrælar (Thralls)</b> Thralls were enslaved people, often captured in raids, who were owned by others and made to work without pay.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Feudal Europe</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/henry-ii-of-england.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Rex (The king)</b> Kings were crowned in church and ruled with the help of great lords, who promised to be loyal to them.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/henry-the-lion.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Magnates (Great lords)</b> Great lords such as dukes and counts lived in castles, ruled large lands and led knights for the king.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/wolfram-von-eschenbach.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Milites (Knights)</b> Knights were warriors who fought on horseback and were given land in return for serving their lord.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/albertus-magnus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Clerici (Priests, monks and nuns)</b> Priests looked after village churches, while monks and nuns lived together in monasteries, praying and working.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/feudal-europe-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Burgenses (Townspeople)</b> Townspeople were merchants and craftspeople, and many towns won the right to run their own markets and councils.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/villein.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Villani (Serfs and villeins)</b> Serfs were farmers who had to work on their lord’s land and could not move away without his permission.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/feudal-europe-10.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Servi (Enslaved people)</b> Enslaved people were owned by others and worked without pay; in England slavery had died out by about 1150.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/venice">the Republic of Venice</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/francesco-foscari.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Doge</b> The doge was the head of Venice, chosen for life, but the councils kept a close watch on everything he did.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/venice-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Patriziato (Patricians)</b> Noble men ran the government of Venice together, and their names were written in a register called the Golden Book.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/venice-3.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Clergy</b> Priests, monks and nuns served Venice’s many churches, but the government kept a close eye on what they said.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murano.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Popolo (Common people)</b> Most Venetians were fishermen, salt workers, shipbuilders, glassmakers and other workers.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/venice-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Jews of the Ghetto</b> Venice’s Jews had to live in one district, the Ghetto, and wear a red cloth, but they had their own synagogues there.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">the Dutch Republic</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/maurice-prince-of-orange.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Stadhouder (The stadtholder)</b> The stadtholder, usually the Prince of Orange, led the army of the Dutch Republic, but the provinces’ assemblies made the laws.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johan-de-witt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Regenten (Regents and patricians)</b> Rich merchant families ran the Dutch cities, and their men sat for life on the town councils.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/synod-of-dort.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Ministers of the Reformed Church</b> Ministers of the Reformed Church preached in plain churches without statues, and only church members could hold public office.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/dutch-republic-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Farmers, craftsmen and shopkeepers</b> Farmers, craftsmen and shopkeepers grew food, made goods and ran shops, and windmills pumped water off new farmland.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/dutch-republic-9.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Skilled labourers, servants and sailors</b> Sailors, maids and workers earned more in the Dutch Republic than in most of Europe.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/dutch-republic-10.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Paupers</b> The poorest people begged or worked by the day, and the towns built almshouses for the old.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/han-china">Han China</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/liu-bang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Huangdi (The emperor)</b> The Han emperor was called the Son of Heaven, and people were not allowed to say his name.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/han-xin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Zhuhou (Kings and marquesses)</b> Han kings were usually relatives of the emperor, and they lived on taxes from the lands they were given.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/xiao-he.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Guan (Officials and scholars)</b> Many Han officials studied the books of Confucius and were chosen for being good sons and honest men.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yuan-shao.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Haozu (Great landed families)</b> Rich Han families owned large farms, and poorer families rented their fields and gave them about half the harvest.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/han-china-5.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Nong (Farmers)</b> Most people in Han China were farmers, and men had to work for the government for one month each year.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/han-china-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Gong (Artisans)</b> Han craftspeople made shiny lacquer cups, bronze mirrors and fine silk cloth.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/han-china-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Shang (Merchants)</b> Some Han merchants grew very rich, but laws kept many of them from wearing silk or working for the government.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/han-china-8.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Nubi (Enslaved people)</b> Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; in Han China there were few of them.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Heian Japan</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/emperor-kanmu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Tennō (The emperor)</b> The emperor lived in the palace at Heian-kyō, today’s Kyoto, and led the court, but his Fujiwara relatives held the real power.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/fujiwara-no-michinaga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Sesshō and kampaku (The Fujiwara regents)</b> The Fujiwara regents married their daughters to emperors and then ruled Japan in the name of their young grandsons.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/heian-japan-3.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Kizoku (Court nobles)</b> Court nobles lived in the capital, wore robes chosen by their office and the season, and wrote poems for every occasion.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/lotus-sutra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Buddhist monks and Shinto priests</b> Buddhist monks lived in great mountain monasteries near the capital and were among the few people who could read and write.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/heian-japan-5.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Jige (Court clerks and menials)</b> Below the nobles worked the court’s clerks, musicians and doctors, who earned far less rice.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/rice.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Ryōmin (Peasants)</b> Most people were farmers who grew rice and gave part of every harvest to the estate owner or the government.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/heian-japan-8.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Senmin (Base people)</b> The old laws made some people “base people”, close to slaves, but by around the year 1000 that line had almost disappeared.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/tokugawa-japan">Tokugawa Japan</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/tokugawa-japan-1.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Kinri and kuge (The emperor and the court nobles)</b> The emperor lived in Kyoto and was greatly honoured, but the shogun, not the emperor, ruled Japan.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tokugawa-ieyasu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Shōgun</b> The shogun was Japan’s real ruler, a general from the Tokugawa family who governed from the city of Edo.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/date-masamune.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Daimyō</b> Daimyo were great lords who ruled parts of Japan for the shogun and had to live in his city every other year.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bushido.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Bushi (Samurai)</b> Samurai were warriors who wore two swords, and in the long Edo peace most of them worked as officials and guards.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/buddhism.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Sōryo (Buddhist and Shinto clergy)</b> Buddhist priests looked after temples and kept a record of every family that belonged to them.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ninomiya-sontoku.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Hyakushō (Peasants)</b> Most people in Edo Japan were farmers who grew rice and paid a large share of it to their lord.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/tokugawa-japan-9.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Kawata (Outcasts of the leather trades)</b> Kawata families did jobs such as leatherwork that others thought unclean, and they were made to live apart.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/khmer-empire">the Khmer Empire</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jayavarman-vii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Devaraja (The king)</b> The kings of Angkor were seen as gods on earth, and they built huge stone temples such as Angkor Wat and the Bayon.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/khmer-empire-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Kshatriya (Royalty, ministers and nobles)</b> Princes and ministers rode on elephants under red umbrellas, and the higher a man’s rank, the bigger his house.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/khmer-empire-3.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Brahmins</b> Brahmins were Hindu priests who led the ceremonies of the king and the nobles.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/khmer-empire-5.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Traders and artisans</b> At Angkor’s market most of the traders were women, who sat on straw mats on the ground to sell their goods.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/khmer-empire-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Rice farmers and fishermen</b> Most Khmer people were rice farmers and fishermen who lived in bamboo houses on stilts, safe from floods.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/angkor-thom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Slaves</b> At the bottom of Khmer society were slaves, people who belonged to others and were not free.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/majapahit">Majapahit</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/raden-wijaya.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>The king</b> The king of Majapahit was seen as a living god, and once a year people from many islands came to bring him tribute.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/majapahit-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Bhre (Royal kin and provincial lords)</b> The king’s close relatives ruled the provinces of Java and helped him make big decisions.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/adityawarman.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Mantri (Ministers, officials and nobles)</b> Ministers helped the king run Majapahit, and the greatest of them, Gajah Mada, swore to unite the islands.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jong-ship.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Prajurit (Professional soldiers)</b> Majapahit had a paid army of 30,000 soldiers and great ships called jongs that carried them across the sea.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/majapahit-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Artisans and traders</b> Craftspeople in Majapahit made gold jewellery, clay pots and money boxes shaped like wild boars.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/majapahit-9.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Servants of the palace and great households</b> Servants lived in rows of houses inside the palace walls and worked for the royal family.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/ayutthaya">the Ayutthaya Kingdom</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/phetracha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Phra Chao Phaendin (The king)</b> The king of Ayutthaya was called Lord of the Land, and people spoke to him in a special royal language.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ayutthaya-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Princes of the royal clan</b> The king’s sons and brothers lived at court, and one of them was chosen to be the next king.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kosa-pan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Mun nai (Officials and lords)</b> Each noble in Ayutthaya had a number that showed his rank, and he led the men who worked and fought for the king.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ayutthaya-4.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Sangha (Monks and court Brahmins)</b> Buddhist monks lived in temples, and any man, rich or poor, could become a monk.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ayutthaya-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Phrai (Commoners)</b> Most people in Ayutthaya were rice farmers, and every man had to work for the king or a lord for part of each year.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ayutthaya-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>That (Enslaved people)</b> Some people in Ayutthaya were slaves who belonged to a master, often because they were captured in war or owed money.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">the Aztec Empire</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huey-tlatoani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Huey tlatoani (The emperor)</b> The huey tlatoani, or “great speaker”, ruled the Aztecs from the island city of Tenochtitlan.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tlacaelel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Pipiltin (Nobles)</b> Aztec nobles were born into their rank, went to temple schools and became generals, officials and priests.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/templo-mayor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Teopixqui (Priests)</b> Aztec priests looked after the temples, taught in the schools and led the festivals for the gods.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pochteca.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Pochteca (Long-distance merchants and luxury craftsmen)</b> Aztec merchants made long journeys to faraway lands to trade for colourful feathers and other treasures.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/macehualtin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Macehualtin (Commoners)</b> Most Aztecs were farmers who grew maize, beans and squash and gave part of what they made to their rulers.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/aztec-empire-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Mayeque (Landless tenants)</b> Some Aztec farmers had no land of their own and worked the fields of a noble instead.</span></li><li><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/aztec-empire-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Tlacotin (Enslaved people)</b> Enslaved people had to work for an owner without pay, but their children were born free.</span></li></ol></article>
