<section class="kd-sec" id="t-egypt" aria-labelledby="t-egypt-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-egypt-h">Ancient Egypt</h2><p class="kd-intro">Pharaohs, pyramids and the gift of the Nile.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="180" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Great Pyramid of Giza</span><span class="kd-card-d">Built about 4,500 years ago, it was the tallest building on Earth for some 3,800 years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/khufu"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khufu.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="519" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Khufu</span><span class="kd-card-d">The pharaoh who built the Great Pyramid; the only complete statue of him ever found is just 7.5 centimetres tall.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii.jpg" alt="" width="280" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ramesses II</span><span class="kd-card-d">He ruled for 66 years and put up giant statues of himself all over Egypt.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hatshepsut.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hatshepsut</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the few women to rule Egypt as pharaoh; some statues show her with a king's false beard.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akhenaten.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Akhenaten</span><span class="kd-card-d">A pharaoh who tried to make Egyptians worship a single sun god, the Aten.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii.jpg" alt="" width="317" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Cleopatra VII</span><span class="kd-card-d">Egypt's last queen spoke many languages and was the first of her family to learn Egyptian.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/nile"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nile.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="214" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Nile</span><span class="kd-card-d">Africa's longest river: its yearly flood left rich black mud for farming.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="374" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Rosetta Stone</span><span class="kd-card-d">The same message in three scripts helped scholars finally read hieroglyphs in 1822.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-simbel.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Abu Simbel</span><span class="kd-card-d">Two temples carved into a cliff, cut into blocks and moved uphill in the 1960s to save them from a new lake.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="235" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Deir el-Bahari</span><span class="kd-card-d">The terraced temple of Queen Hatshepsut, built against the cliffs near Luxor.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/rhind-mathematical-papyrus"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhind-mathematical-papyrus.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="192" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Rhind Mathematical Papyrus</span><span class="kd-card-d">An Egyptian maths book about 3,600 years old, full of puzzles about bread, beer and pyramids.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-egypt">Play the Ancient Egypt Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-greece" aria-labelledby="t-greece-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-greece-h">Ancient Greece</h2><p class="kd-intro">Thinkers, athletes and the first democracy.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parthenon.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="225" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Parthenon</span><span class="kd-card-d">A marble temple for the goddess Athena, built on a hill above Athens nearly 2,500 years ago.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/athens"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athens.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="180" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Athens</span><span class="kd-card-d">The city where citizens first voted on their own laws, in a democracy.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonidas-i.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="464" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Leonidas I</span><span class="kd-card-d">A king of Sparta who led his 300 Spartans at the pass of Thermopylae in 480 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/homer"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/homer.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Homer</span><span class="kd-card-d">The poet said to have told the stories of the Trojan War and of Odysseus' long voyage home.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/socrates"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/socrates.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Socrates</span><span class="kd-card-d">He taught by asking questions, and he never wrote a single book.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/plato"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plato.jpg" alt="" width="280" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Plato</span><span class="kd-card-d">A pupil of Socrates who started one of the first schools, the Academy.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotle.jpg" alt="" width="314" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Aristotle</span><span class="kd-card-d">He studied everything from animals and plants to poetry, and he taught Alexander the Great.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Alexander the Great</span><span class="kd-card-d">By the age of 30 he ruled an empire that stretched from Greece to India.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ancient-olympic-games"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ancient-olympic-games.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="212" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ancient Olympic Games</span><span class="kd-card-d">Held at Olympia every four years for more than a thousand years, with races, wrestling and chariots.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/colossus-of-rhodes"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colossus-of-rhodes.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="437" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Colossus of Rhodes</span><span class="kd-card-d">A bronze statue of the sun god about as tall as a ten-storey building; an earthquake toppled it.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="272" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Lighthouse of Alexandria</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the Seven Wonders, its fire guided ships into harbour for many centuries.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-greece">Play the Ancient Greece Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-rome" aria-labelledby="t-rome-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-rome-h">Ancient Rome</h2><p class="kd-intro">Emperors, legions and roads that led everywhere.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="" width="309" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Julius Caesar</span><span class="kd-card-d">A famous general who became master of Rome; the month of July is named after him.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/cicero"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cicero.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="480" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Cicero</span><span class="kd-card-d">Rome's greatest speaker; hundreds of his letters still survive.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hannibal.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="387" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hannibal</span><span class="kd-card-d">Rome's great enemy, who marched an army with elephants across the Alps.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/roman-forum"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-forum.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="131" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Roman Forum</span><span class="kd-card-d">The busy heart of the city, full of temples, law courts and shops.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/appian-way.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Appian Way</span><span class="kd-card-d">A stone road begun in 312 BCE; you can still walk along parts of it today.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/trajan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trajan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="417" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Trajan</span><span class="kd-card-d">Under this emperor the Roman Empire reached its largest size.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Marcus Aurelius</span><span class="kd-card-d">An emperor who wrote his thoughts in a notebook we still read, the Meditations.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/nero"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nero.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="435" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Nero</span><span class="kd-card-d">An emperor who loved music and theatre, and even sang on stage himself.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pompeii.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="197" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Pompeii</span><span class="kd-card-d">A town buried by the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 CE, so well preserved that you can walk its streets.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-i.jpg" alt="" width="293" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Constantine I</span><span class="kd-card-d">The emperor who founded Constantinople, the city we now call Istanbul.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-rome">Play the Ancient Rome Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-knights" aria-labelledby="t-knights-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-knights-h">Knights and Castles</h2><p class="kd-intro">Kings, queens and castles of the Middle Ages.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="533" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">William the Conqueror</span><span class="kd-card-d">He sailed from Normandy and won the Battle of Hastings in 1066.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux-tapestry"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayeux-tapestry.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="232" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Bayeux Tapestry</span><span class="kd-card-d">A 70-metre-long embroidery that tells the story of 1066 like a comic strip.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="180" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Eleanor of Aquitaine</span><span class="kd-card-d">She was queen of France and then queen of England, and the mother of two English kings.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="180" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Richard I of England</span><span class="kd-card-d">Called the Lionheart, he spent only a few months of his reign in England.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/knights-templar.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="192" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Knights Templar</span><span class="kd-card-d">An order of knights who were also monks, dressed in white with a red cross.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/krak-des-chevaliers"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krak-des-chevaliers.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="214" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Krak des Chevaliers</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the best-preserved castles of the Crusades, high on a hill in Syria.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="477" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Joan of Arc</span><span class="kd-card-d">A farm girl from France who led an army when she was about 17.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magna-carta.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Magna Carta</span><span class="kd-card-d">A charter of 1215 which said that even the king had to obey the law.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-knights">Play the Knights and Castles Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-vikings" aria-labelledby="t-vikings-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-vikings-h">Vikings</h2><p class="kd-intro">Sailors, traders and explorers from the far north.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/leif-erikson"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leif-erikson.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="480" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Leif Erikson</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Viking explorer who reached North America about 500 years before Columbus.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erik-the-red.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="453" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Erik the Red</span><span class="kd-card-d">He named his icy new home Greenland to make settlers want to go there.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/thor"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thor.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="425" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Thor</span><span class="kd-card-d">The Norse god of thunder; Thursday is named after him.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="230" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Oseberg ship</span><span class="kd-card-d">A beautifully carved Viking ship found buried in a grave mound in Norway.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/gokstad-ship"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gokstad-ship.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Gokstad ship</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Viking ship so well made that a copy sailed across the Atlantic in 1893.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">L'Anse aux Meadows</span><span class="kd-card-d">The remains of a Viking settlement in Canada, about a thousand years old.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/froso-runestone"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/froso-runestone.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="651" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Frösö Runestone</span><span class="kd-card-d">A stone carved with runes, the letters the Vikings wrote with.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lindisfarne.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Lindisfarne</span><span class="kd-card-d">An island monastery in England whose raid by Vikings in 793 shocked Europe.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-vikings">Play the Vikings Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-explorers" aria-labelledby="t-explorers-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-explorers-h">Great Explorers</h2><p class="kd-intro">Brave travellers who crossed oceans, deserts and mountains.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xuanzang.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="716" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Xuanzang</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Chinese monk who walked all the way to India and back to collect books.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marco-polo.jpg" alt="" width="245" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Marco Polo</span><span class="kd-card-d">A merchant from Venice who travelled to China and wrote a famous book about it.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta.jpg" alt="" width="291" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ibn Battuta</span><span class="kd-card-d">He travelled for nearly 30 years across Africa and Asia, further than anyone else of his time.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zheng-he.jpg" alt="" width="280" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Zheng He</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Chinese admiral who led huge fleets across the Indian Ocean; his treasure ships are said to be among the largest wooden ships ever built.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="409" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Prince Henry the Navigator</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Portuguese prince who sent ships down the coast of Africa, though he rarely sailed himself.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="387" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Christopher Columbus</span><span class="kd-card-d">In 1492 he sailed across the Atlantic, hoping to reach Asia.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama.jpg" alt="" width="309" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Vasco da Gama</span><span class="kd-card-d">The first European to sail all the way from Europe to India, in 1498.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="395" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ferdinand Magellan</span><span class="kd-card-d">His fleet was the first to sail around the whole world, though he died on the way.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-cook.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="401" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">James Cook</span><span class="kd-card-d">A British captain who mapped the coasts of New Zealand and eastern Australia.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-explorers">Play the Great Explorers Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-inventions" aria-labelledby="t-inventions-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-inventions-h">Inventions and Ideas</h2><p class="kd-intro">Clever ideas that changed how people live.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi.jpg" alt="" width="255" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Code of Hammurabi</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the oldest written law codes, carved on a tall black stone nearly 3,800 years ago.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="325" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Library of Alexandria</span><span class="kd-card-d">An ancient library that tried to collect a copy of every book it could find.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/archimedes"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/archimedes.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Archimedes</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Greek inventor who is said to have shouted &quot;Eureka!&quot; in his bath.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/euclid"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euclid.jpg" alt="" width="311" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Euclid</span><span class="kd-card-d">His geometry book was used in schools for more than 2,000 years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/cai-lun"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cai-lun.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="400" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Cai Lun</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Chinese court official remembered for improving paper-making around the year 105.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">al-Khwarizmi</span><span class="kd-card-d">A scholar in Baghdad whose name gave us the word &quot;algorithm&quot;.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="325" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">House of Wisdom</span><span class="kd-card-d">A great library and meeting place for scholars in Baghdad.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Johannes Gutenberg</span><span class="kd-card-d">His printing press with movable metal letters made books far cheaper.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="459" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Leonardo da Vinci</span><span class="kd-card-d">A painter and inventor who filled notebooks with designs for flying machines.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="406" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Galileo Galilei</span><span class="kd-card-d">In 1610 he looked through his telescope and discovered four moons of Jupiter.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isaac-newton.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="385" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Isaac Newton</span><span class="kd-card-d">He explained gravity, the force that makes apples fall and keeps the Moon in orbit.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-inventions">Play the Inventions and Ideas Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-buildings" aria-labelledby="t-buildings-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-buildings-h">Amazing Buildings</h2><p class="kd-intro">Wonders of stone, brick and marble from around the world.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="212" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Great Wall of China</span><span class="kd-card-d">Built and rebuilt over 2,000 years, its walls run for thousands of kilometres.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taj-mahal.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="211" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Taj Mahal</span><span class="kd-card-d">A white marble tomb built by the emperor Shah Jahan for his wife, Mumtaz Mahal.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angkor-wat.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="218" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Angkor Wat</span><span class="kd-card-d">A vast temple in Cambodia, often called the largest religious monument in the world.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/borobudur.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Borobudur</span><span class="kd-card-d">A giant Buddhist monument on the island of Java with more than 500 Buddha statues.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="193" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hagia Sophia</span><span class="kd-card-d">For nearly a thousand years it was the largest church in the world.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alhambra.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Alhambra</span><span class="kd-card-d">A palace in Granada famous for its gardens, fountains and patterned walls.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/machu-picchu.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Machu Picchu</span><span class="kd-card-d">An Inca city high in the Andes mountains, built around 1450.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/petra"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petra.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Petra</span><span class="kd-card-d">A city carved into pink sandstone cliffs in Jordan.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="180" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Pyramid of the Sun</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the largest pyramids in the world, in the ancient city of Teotihuacan in Mexico.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="140" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ziggurat of Ur</span><span class="kd-card-d">A stepped temple tower built about 4,000 years ago in what is now Iraq.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-minar"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qutb-minar.jpg" alt="" width="278" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Qutb Minar</span><span class="kd-card-d">A red sandstone tower in Delhi about 73 metres tall, begun around the year 1200.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hanging-gardens-of-babylon"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanging-gardens-of-babylon.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="221" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hanging Gardens of Babylon</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the Seven Wonders; no one has ever found it, and some think it was only a legend.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-buildings">Play the Amazing Buildings Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-china" aria-labelledby="t-china-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-china-h">Ancient China</h2><p class="kd-intro">Emperors, inventors and a wall across the mountains.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="212" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Great Wall of China</span><span class="kd-card-d">Guards in its watchtowers sent smoke and fire signals to warn that raiders were coming.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="431" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Qin Shi Huang</span><span class="kd-card-d">China's first emperor made everyone use the same coins, the same weights and the same writing.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/confucius"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/confucius.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="299" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Confucius</span><span class="kd-card-d">A teacher who lived about 2,500 years ago and taught people to be kind, honest and fair.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yangtze-river.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="212" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Yangtze River</span><span class="kd-card-d">Asia's longest river, about 6,300 kilometres long, flowing from the mountains of Tibet to the sea.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yellow-river.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="198" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Yellow River</span><span class="kd-card-d">Its water carries so much yellow mud that the river is named after its colour.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/changan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/changan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="372" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Chang'an</span><span class="kd-card-d">About 1,300 years ago it was one of the biggest cities in the world, with streets laid out in a neat grid.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luoyang.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Luoyang</span><span class="kd-card-d">Near this old capital, people carved tens of thousands of Buddha statues into the cliffs of the Longmen Grottoes.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dunhuang.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Dunhuang</span><span class="kd-card-d">A desert oasis on the Silk Road with hundreds of caves full of colourful paintings.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zheng-he.jpg" alt="" width="280" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Zheng He</span><span class="kd-card-d">An admiral who sailed seven times with giant treasure ships, reaching as far as East Africa.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kublai-khan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="399" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Kublai Khan</span><span class="kd-card-d">The grandson of Genghis Khan, he became emperor of China and built a new capital where Beijing stands today.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-china">Play the Ancient China Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-africa" aria-labelledby="t-africa-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-africa-h">Africa's Great Kingdoms</h2><p class="kd-intro">Golden empires, trading cities and builders in stone.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mansa-musa.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="365" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Mansa Musa</span><span class="kd-card-d">The ruler of Mali whose journey to Mecca in 1324 carried so much gold that people talked about it for centuries.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timbuktu.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="220" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Timbuktu</span><span class="kd-card-d">A city at the edge of the Sahara where scholars wrote and copied thousands of books.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Great Zimbabwe</span><span class="kd-card-d">Its great curving walls were built from blocks of granite without any mortar.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes.jpg" alt="" width="302" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Benin Bronzes</span><span class="kd-card-d">Artists of the Kingdom of Benin made hundreds of brass plaques and heads for the royal palace.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lalibela.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="277" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Lalibela</span><span class="kd-card-d">Eleven churches here were carved down into solid rock about 800 years ago.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/meroe"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meroe.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="154" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Meroë</span><span class="kd-card-d">The royal cemeteries of this city of the Kingdom of Kush hold around 200 small, steep pyramids.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Kilwa Kisiwani</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Swahili island town whose sultans grew rich trading gold from inland Africa.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Djinguereber Mosque</span><span class="kd-card-d">A mosque of mud brick in Timbuktu, built around 1327, that is given a fresh coat of mud every year.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/njinga-of-ndongo-and-matamba"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/njinga-of-ndongo-and-matamba.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="426" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Njinga of Ndongo and Matamba</span><span class="kd-card-d">Queen Njinga ruled in Angola for decades and fought hard to keep her lands free of Portuguese rule.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-africa">Play the Africa's Great Kingdoms Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-americas" aria-labelledby="t-americas-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-americas-h">Aztecs, Maya and Inca</h2><p class="kd-intro">Cities of the sun, the jaguar and the high Andes.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/machu-picchu.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Machu Picchu</span><span class="kd-card-d">Built for an Inca emperor around 1450 on a mountain ridge about 2,400 metres high.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="176" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Sacsayhuamán</span><span class="kd-card-d">On a hill above the Inca capital of Cusco, huge stone blocks were cut to fit so tightly that a knife blade cannot slip between them.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pachacuti.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="349" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Pachacuti</span><span class="kd-card-d">The Inca ruler who began turning a small kingdom into the largest empire in the Americas.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/templo-mayor.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Templo Mayor</span><span class="kd-card-d">The Aztecs' great temple had two shrines on top, one for the god of rain and one for the god of war.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/kinich-janaab-pakal"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kinich-janaab-pakal.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="412" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">K'inich Janaab Pakal</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Maya king of Palenque who was buried with a mask made of green jade.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atahualpa.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="413" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Atahualpa</span><span class="kd-card-d">The last ruler of the Inca Empire, captured by the Spanish in 1532.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/nazca-lines"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nazca-lines.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Nazca Lines</span><span class="kd-card-d">Giant drawings of animals scratched into the desert of Peru, best seen from the air.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii.jpg" alt="" width="261" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Moctezuma II</span><span class="kd-card-d">The Aztec emperor who met Hernán Cortés in 1519.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-americas">Play the Aztecs, Maya and Inca Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-pacific" aria-labelledby="t-pacific-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-pacific-h">Islands and Ocean Voyagers</h2><p class="kd-intro">Star-guided canoes, sacred kings and stone giants of the Pacific.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/easter-island.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Easter Island</span><span class="kd-card-d">Its people carved nearly 900 giant stone statues called moai.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="456" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Kamehameha I</span><span class="kd-card-d">A warrior king who united all the islands of Hawaii by 1810.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polynesians.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Polynesians</span><span class="kd-card-d">Polynesian sailors crossed thousands of kilometres of open ocean, steering by the stars, the waves and the birds.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/taputapuatea"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taputapuatea.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="161" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Taputapuatea</span><span class="kd-card-d">A great stone temple in French Polynesia where voyagers from many islands once gathered.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/haamonga-a-maui"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haamonga-a-maui.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Haʻamonga ʻa Maui</span><span class="kd-card-d">A stone gateway in Tonga made of three huge blocks of coral rock.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/samoan-islands"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samoan-islands.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="217" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Samoan Islands</span><span class="kd-card-d">Samoans are famous for their fine woven mats, their songs and their councils of chiefs.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-cook.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="401" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">James Cook</span><span class="kd-card-d">A British explorer who sailed the Pacific three times and drew maps of many of its islands.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/native-hawaiians"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/native-hawaiians.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Native Hawaiians</span><span class="kd-card-d">Hula, the Hawaiian dance, tells stories with movements of the hands, the hips and the feet.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/nukualofa"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nukualofa.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Nukuʻalofa</span><span class="kd-card-d">Tonga's capital, where the king's white wooden palace has stood by the sea since 1867.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-pacific">Play the Islands and Ocean Voyagers Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-india" aria-labelledby="t-india-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-india-h">India's Golden Ages</h2><p class="kd-intro">Emperors, mathematicians and monuments of the Indian subcontinent.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashoka.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="305" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ashoka</span><span class="kd-card-d">An emperor who gave up war and had messages of kindness carved on rocks and pillars across India.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/akbar"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akbar.jpg" alt="" width="311" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Akbar</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Mughal emperor who loved to hear people of different religions talk about their beliefs.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nalanda.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="202" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Nalanda</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the world's first great universities, where students came from as far away as China and Korea.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ajanta-caves"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ajanta-caves.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ajanta Caves</span><span class="kd-card-d">Caves carved into a cliff and painted with stories of the Buddha more than 1,500 years ago.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/aryabhata"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aryabhata.jpg" alt="" width="293" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Aryabhata</span><span class="kd-card-d">A mathematician who worked out more than 1,500 years ago that the Earth spins on its axis.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalidasa.jpg" alt="" width="293" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Kalidasa</span><span class="kd-card-d">India's most famous classical poet, who wrote plays and poems in Sanskrit.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/delhi"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/delhi.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="380" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Delhi</span><span class="kd-card-d">A city that has been a capital again and again for more than 800 years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="404" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Chandragupta Maurya</span><span class="kd-card-d">He founded the Maurya Empire, the first empire to rule most of India.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-jahan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="463" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Shah Jahan</span><span class="kd-card-d">The Mughal emperor who built the Taj Mahal in memory of his wife, Mumtaz Mahal.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i.jpg" alt="" width="303" height="353" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Rajendra Chola I</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Chola king whose fleet sailed across the sea to Southeast Asia.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-india">Play the India's Golden Ages Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-islamic" aria-labelledby="t-islamic-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-islamic-h">The Islamic Golden Age</h2><p class="kd-intro">Scholars, poets and travellers of a world stretching from Spain to Central Asia.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/samarra"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samarra.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Samarra</span><span class="kd-card-d">An Abbasid capital on the River Tigris, famous for the great spiral minaret of its mosque, built in the 800s.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="325" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">House of Wisdom</span><span class="kd-card-d">A great library in Baghdad where scholars translated books from Greek, Persian and Sanskrit into Arabic.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alhambra.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Alhambra</span><span class="kd-card-d">Its walls are covered with patterns and lines of poetry carved in plaster.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="212" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba</span><span class="kd-card-d">A forest of more than 800 columns holding up red-and-white striped arches.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/averroes"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/averroes.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="344" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Averroes</span><span class="kd-card-d">A thinker from Córdoba whose writings about Aristotle were read in Europe for centuries.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta.jpg" alt="" width="291" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ibn Battuta</span><span class="kd-card-d">A traveller from Morocco who journeyed for nearly 30 years across Africa and Asia.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">al-Khwarizmi</span><span class="kd-card-d">A mathematician whose book about algebra was studied in Europe for hundreds of years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="216" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Dome of the Rock</span><span class="kd-card-d">A shrine in Jerusalem with a golden dome, finished around 691.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samarkand.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Samarkand</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Silk Road city famous for its blue-tiled mosques and schools.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isfahan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="227" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Isfahan</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Persian city with a vast square that was once used for games of polo.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg.jpg" alt="" width="314" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ulugh Beg</span><span class="kd-card-d">A ruler of Samarkand who built an observatory and measured the length of the year very precisely.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-islamic">Play the The Islamic Golden Age Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-japan" aria-labelledby="t-japan-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-japan-h">Samurai and Old Japan</h2><p class="kd-intro">Warriors, castles and poets of the islands of the rising sun.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/samurai"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samurai.jpg" alt="" width="280" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Samurai</span><span class="kd-card-d">Japan's warriors, trained in archery, riding and the sword.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kyoto.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Kyoto</span><span class="kd-card-d">Japan's capital for more than a thousand years, full of temples and gardens.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/shinto"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shinto.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Shinto</span><span class="kd-card-d">Japan's oldest religion, which honours the kami, the spirits of nature.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="365" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Tokugawa Ieyasu</span><span class="kd-card-d">The shogun who founded a family that ruled Japan in peace for more than 250 years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ninja"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ninja.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="593" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ninja</span><span class="kd-card-d">Spies of old Japan who gathered secrets; many stories about them are legends.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/mount-fuji"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-fuji.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="156" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Mount Fuji</span><span class="kd-card-d">Japan's highest mountain, a volcano 3,776 metres high.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="350" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Oda Nobunaga</span><span class="kd-card-d">A warlord who began to unite Japan in the 1500s.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="320" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</span><span class="kd-card-d">He rose from a poor family to become the ruler of all Japan.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-japan">Play the Samurai and Old Japan Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-mesopotamia" aria-labelledby="t-mesopotamia-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-mesopotamia-h">Ancient Mesopotamia</h2><p class="kd-intro">The land between two rivers, where the first cities and writing began.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hammurabi.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="353" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hammurabi</span><span class="kd-card-d">A king of Babylon who had his laws carved on a tall stone for everyone to see.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="140" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ziggurat of Ur</span><span class="kd-card-d">Its builders used millions of mud bricks, some stamped with the king's name.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ishtar-gate"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ishtar-gate.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="199" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ishtar Gate</span><span class="kd-card-d">A gate of Babylon covered in blue glazed bricks with pictures of bulls and dragons.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/babylon"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babylon.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Babylon</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the greatest cities of the ancient world, on the banks of the Euphrates.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nineveh.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="219" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Nineveh</span><span class="kd-card-d">A great Assyrian city whose king kept a library of clay tablets.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ashurbanipal</span><span class="kd-card-d">An Assyrian king who collected thousands of clay tablets, among them the story of Gilgamesh.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="362" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Epic of Gilgamesh</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the oldest stories in the world, about a king who searched for everlasting life.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/standard-of-ur"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/standard-of-ur.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Standard of Ur</span><span class="kd-card-d">A box decorated with shell and blue stone, showing scenes of war and peace from about 4,500 years ago.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/tigris"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tigris.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Tigris</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the two great rivers that gave Mesopotamia its name: the land between the rivers.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euphrates.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Euphrates</span><span class="kd-card-d">The river beside which Babylon was built.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-mesopotamia">Play the Ancient Mesopotamia Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-mongols" aria-labelledby="t-mongols-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-mongols-h">The Mongol Empire</h2><p class="kd-intro">Riders of the steppe who built an empire stretching from Korea to eastern Europe.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="406" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Genghis Khan</span><span class="kd-card-d">Born with the name Temüjin, he united the Mongol tribes in 1206 and founded the Mongol Empire.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kublai-khan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="399" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Kublai Khan</span><span class="kd-card-d">A grandson of Genghis Khan who became Great Khan and founded the Yuan Dynasty in China.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/batu-khan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="443" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Batu Khan</span><span class="kd-card-d">Another grandson of Genghis Khan, he ruled the western steppe from his capital, Sarai, on the River Volga.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karakorum.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="179" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Karakorum</span><span class="kd-card-d">The capital of the Mongol Empire in central Mongolia, where the Great Khan had his seat.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khanbaliq.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Khanbaliq</span><span class="kd-card-d">Its name means 'city of the khan': Kublai Khan built it where Beijing stands today, laid out on a neat grid.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/shangdu"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shangdu.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Shangdu</span><span class="kd-card-d">Kublai Khan's summer capital, known in the West as Xanadu; Marco Polo described its palace and park.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/horse-archer.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="308" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Horse archer</span><span class="kd-card-d">Warriors who shot with a bow from horseback, moving fast: the hallmark of armies of the steppe.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/pax-mongolica"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pax-mongolica.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="231" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Pax Mongolica</span><span class="kd-card-d">Under Mongol rule merchants were promised safe travel, so goods and ideas moved between China, the Middle East and Europe.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Secret History of the Mongols</span><span class="kd-card-d">The oldest book of Mongolian literature that still survives, telling the story of young Temüjin.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/tengrism"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tengrism.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="482" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Tengrism</span><span class="kd-card-d">The old religion of the Mongol and Turkic peoples, centred on Tengri, the god of the sky.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/gobi-desert"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gobi-desert.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Gobi Desert</span><span class="kd-card-d">A large desert that stretches across Mongolia and northern China.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-mongols">Play the The Mongol Empire Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-persia" aria-labelledby="t-persia-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-persia-h">Ancient Persia</h2><p class="kd-intro">Great kings, royal roads and palaces of ancient Iran.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great.jpg" alt="" width="261" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Cyrus the Great</span><span class="kd-card-d">He founded the Achaemenid Empire and took the great city of Babylon in 539 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="193" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Cyrus Cylinder</span><span class="kd-card-d">A clay cylinder written in cuneiform that tells of temples being restored after Cyrus took Babylon.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-i.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="400" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Darius I</span><span class="kd-card-d">A king who divided his empire into provinces and brought in coins called darics.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/persepolis.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="212" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Persepolis</span><span class="kd-card-d">A grand capital whose carvings show peoples from across the empire bringing tribute to the king.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/apadana"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apadana.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Apadana</span><span class="kd-card-d">A huge hall at Persepolis whose roof rested on 72 stone columns, each about 20 metres high.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/susa"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/susa.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="212" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Susa</span><span class="kd-card-d">Once the capital of Elam, it became a royal capital of the Persian kings, at the eastern end of the Royal Road.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Behistun Inscription</span><span class="kd-card-d">Words carved on a cliff in three languages, which helped scholars learn to read cuneiform.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Naqsh-e Rostam</span><span class="kd-card-d">The tombs of Persian kings, cut into the rock near Persepolis.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/qanat"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qanat.jpg" alt="" width="300" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Qanat</span><span class="kd-card-d">An underground channel that carries water from the mountains down to the fields, first made in ancient Iran.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/fire-temple"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fire-temple.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Fire temple</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Zoroastrian place of worship, where a sacred fire is kept burning.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ardashir-i.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="321" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ardashir I</span><span class="kd-card-d">The king who founded the Sasanian Empire, whose family ruled Iran for over four centuries.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khosrow-i.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="320" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Khosrow I</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Sasanian king who supported learning; later Persian tradition remembered him as a just king.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-persia">Play the Ancient Persia Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-silk-road" aria-labelledby="t-silk-road-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-silk-road-h">The Silk Road</h2><p class="kd-intro">Merchants, monks and caravans on the trade routes linking China with the West.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/silk"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silk.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="480" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Silk</span><span class="kd-card-d">A fine, strong cloth made from the cocoons of silkworms, first made in China: it gave the Silk Road its name.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-qian.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="233" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Zhang Qian</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Han envoy sent west around 138 BCE, whose reports opened the way for the Silk Road.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="193" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Gansu Corridor</span><span class="kd-card-d">A narrow strip of land between mountains and desert that linked China with Central Asia; its capture by Han China opened the Silk Road.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/mogao-caves"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mogao-caves.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Mogao Caves</span><span class="kd-card-d">Nearly 500 Buddhist cave temples cut into a cliff, full of some of the finest paintings and statues in Asia.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kashgar.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Kashgar</span><span class="kd-card-d">An oasis city in far western China where Silk Road routes over the Pamirs met the routes around the Tarim Basin.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Pamir Mountains</span><span class="kd-card-d">Mountains with peaks above 7,000 metres, known as the 'Roof of the World' and crossed by branches of the Silk Road.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/sogdians"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sogdians.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="152" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Sogdians</span><span class="kd-card-d">A people of Central Asia from Samarkand and Bukhara, famous as merchants on the Silk Road.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bukhara.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="153" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Bukhara</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the great oasis cities of the Silk Road and a centre of Islamic learning.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/merv"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merv.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Merv</span><span class="kd-card-d">A great oasis city on the Silk Road, in what is now Turkmenistan.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caravanserai.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Caravanserai</span><span class="kd-card-d">A roadside inn built around a courtyard, where travellers and their animals could stay safely.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palmyra.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="216" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Palmyra</span><span class="kd-card-d">An oasis city in the Syrian desert that grew rich on the caravan trade.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-silk-road">Play the The Silk Road Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-byzantium" aria-labelledby="t-byzantium-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-byzantium-h">The Byzantine Empire</h2><p class="kd-intro">The Roman Empire of the east, ruled from Constantinople for over a thousand years.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="193" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hagia Sophia</span><span class="kd-card-d">Finished in 537, this great domed church was the cathedral of the Byzantine Empire for centuries.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/justinian-i.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Justinian I</span><span class="kd-card-d">An emperor who gathered Roman law into one great collection and built the Hagia Sophia.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-justinian-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-justinian-i.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="437" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Theodora (wife of Justinian I)</span><span class="kd-card-d">A former actress who married Justinian and became one of the most influential women in Byzantine history.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/walls-of-constantinople"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walls-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Walls of Constantinople</span><span class="kd-card-d">A moat, an outer wall and an inner wall, one behind the other, kept the city safe for centuries.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hippodrome-of-constantinople"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hippodrome-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="236" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hippodrome of Constantinople</span><span class="kd-card-d">The city's chariot-racing stadium; two obelisks still stand where it once was.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/solidus"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solidus.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="320" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Solidus</span><span class="kd-card-d">A gold coin that kept the same weight and purity for centuries, used in trade all around the Mediterranean.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius.jpg" alt="" width="317" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Cyril and Methodius</span><span class="kd-card-d">Two missionary brothers who created an alphabet for the Slavs and translated church texts into their language.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-athos.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Mount Athos</span><span class="kd-card-d">A mountain in Greece with twenty Orthodox monasteries; the first large one was founded in 963.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Theodosius II</span><span class="kd-card-d">He became emperor as a child; in his reign the great land walls of Constantinople were finished.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basil-ii.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="397" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Basil II</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the most successful emperors: when he died in 1025 the empire was larger than it had been since the 600s.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos.jpg" alt="" width="279" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Alexios I Komnenos</span><span class="kd-card-d">An emperor who rebuilt the empire's strength after defeats and asked Western Europe for help.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-byzantium">Play the The Byzantine Empire Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-celts" aria-labelledby="t-celts-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-celts-h">The Celts</h2><p class="kd-intro">Chiefs, queens, saints and storytellers of the Iron Age and after.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/gundestrup-cauldron"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gundestrup-cauldron.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="238" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Gundestrup cauldron</span><span class="kd-card-d">A silver cauldron 69 centimetres across, found in pieces in a peat bog in Denmark in 1891.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/boudica"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boudica.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Boudica</span><span class="kd-card-d">A queen of the Iceni in eastern Britain who led a great revolt against Roman rule.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vercingetorix.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="320" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Vercingetorix</span><span class="kd-card-d">A chief who united most of the Gauls against Julius Caesar in 52 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caratacus.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="374" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Caratacus</span><span class="kd-card-d">A British chieftain who fought the Romans; the emperor Claudius spared his life.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/bibracte"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bibracte.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Bibracte</span><span class="kd-card-d">A large walled town of the Gauls on a hill in Burgundy, dug up today by archaeologists.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/maiden-castle-dorset"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maiden-castle-dorset.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="245" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Maiden Castle, Dorset</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the largest Iron Age hillforts in Europe, ringed by several lines of massive earth banks called ramparts.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/broch"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/broch.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Broch</span><span class="kd-card-d">An Iron Age stone tower of Scotland with stairs and galleries hidden inside its hollow double walls.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/picts"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/picts.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Picts</span><span class="kd-card-d">A people of northern and eastern Scotland who left stones carved with symbols.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hill-of-tara"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hill-of-tara.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="400" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hill of Tara</span><span class="kd-card-d">A low ridge in Ireland; the title king of Tara came to mean high king of Ireland.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/iona"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iona.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Iona</span><span class="kd-card-d">A small Scottish island where Columba founded a monastery in 563.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/columba"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/columba.jpg" alt="" width="300" height="447" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Columba</span><span class="kd-card-d">An Irish monk who, with his monks from Iona, spread Christianity among the Picts.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-kells"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-kells.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="417" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Book of Kells</span><span class="kd-card-d">A book of the four Gospels made around 800, decorated with interlace patterns, animals and people.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-celts">Play the The Celts Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-renaissance" aria-labelledby="t-renaissance-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-renaissance-h">The Renaissance</h2><p class="kd-intro">Painters, sculptors and scholars who brought back the learning of Greece and Rome.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="214" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Florence Cathedral</span><span class="kd-card-d">Its eight-sided dome, finished in 1436, was the largest built since ancient times.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="400" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Filippo Brunelleschi</span><span class="kd-card-d">The architect of Florence's great dome, built from 1420 to 1436 without wooden supports underneath.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michelangelo.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="370" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Michelangelo</span><span class="kd-card-d">A sculptor and painter who carved the marble David and painted the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="434" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Sistine Chapel</span><span class="kd-card-d">The Pope's chapel in Vatican City, whose ceiling Michelangelo painted between 1508 and 1512.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/the-last-supper-leonardo"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-last-supper-leonardo.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="160" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">The Last Supper (Leonardo)</span><span class="kd-card-d">Leonardo da Vinci painted this famous meal on the wall of a convent in Milan, around 1495 to 1498.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/raphael"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raphael.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="436" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Raphael</span><span class="kd-card-d">A painter from Urbino who painted The School of Athens on the walls of rooms in the Vatican.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/sandro-botticelli"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sandro-botticelli.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="350" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Sandro Botticelli</span><span class="kd-card-d">A painter of Florence, famous for The Birth of Venus and Primavera.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/donatello"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/donatello.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="400" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Donatello</span><span class="kd-card-d">A sculptor of Florence who worked in marble, bronze and wood.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-medici.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="373" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">House of Medici</span><span class="kd-card-d">A family of bankers who led Florence from 1434 and later ruled as grand dukes of Tuscany.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petrarch.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="403" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Petrarch</span><span class="kd-card-d">A poet whose search for old classical manuscripts made him a founder of Renaissance humanism, the new love of ancient learning.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-durer"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albrecht-durer.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="451" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Albrecht Dürer</span><span class="kd-card-d">A German artist from Nuremberg whose prints, such as his Rhinoceros, made him famous across Europe.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erasmus.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="453" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Erasmus</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Dutch scholar, the most famous of the Northern Renaissance, who wrote The Praise of Folly.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-renaissance">Play the The Renaissance Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-ottomans" aria-labelledby="t-ottomans-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-ottomans-h">The Ottoman Empire</h2><p class="kd-intro">Sultans, great mosques and a capital on the Bosphorus.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/osman-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/osman-i.jpg" alt="" width="263" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Osman I</span><span class="kd-card-d">A chieftain who founded the Ottoman family of rulers, around the year 1300.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/orhan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orhan.jpg" alt="" width="251" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Orhan</span><span class="kd-card-d">Osman's son, who captured Bursa in 1326 and made it the first proper Ottoman capital.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/edirne"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edirne.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Edirne</span><span class="kd-card-d">A city that was the Ottoman capital before 1453, home of the Selimiye Mosque by the architect Sinan.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/walls-of-constantinople"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walls-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Walls of Constantinople</span><span class="kd-card-d">A moat and two lines of walls kept Constantinople safe for centuries, until Ottoman cannons broke through in 1453.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii.jpg" alt="" width="311" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Mehmed II</span><span class="kd-card-d">Known as the Conqueror, he took Constantinople in 1453 and made it his capital.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="193" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hagia Sophia</span><span class="kd-card-d">Finished as a church in 537, it was turned into a mosque after the Ottomans took the city in 1453.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/istanbul.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="155" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Istanbul</span><span class="kd-card-d">The city on the Bosporus, once called Constantinople, was the Ottoman capital until the empire's end.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/selim-i.jpg" alt="" width="253" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Selim I</span><span class="kd-card-d">A sultan who brought Syria, Egypt and the holy cities of Arabia under Ottoman rule.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="374" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Suleiman the Magnificent</span><span class="kd-card-d">Sultan from 1520 to 1566; in Turkish he is called Kanuni, the Lawgiver.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/suleymaniye-mosque"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suleymaniye-mosque.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="239" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Süleymaniye Mosque</span><span class="kd-card-d">A great mosque in Istanbul, built for Suleiman by the architect Mimar Sinan in the 1550s.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa.jpg" alt="" width="236" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hayreddin Barbarossa</span><span class="kd-card-d">A corsair, or sea raider, who became grand admiral of the Ottoman fleet.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/janissaries.jpg" alt="" width="278" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Janissaries</span><span class="kd-card-d">The sultan's elite foot soldiers, one of the first armies in Europe to use firearms on a large scale.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-ottomans">Play the The Ottoman Empire Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-industrial" aria-labelledby="t-industrial-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-industrial-h">The Industrial Revolution</h2><p class="kd-intro">Steam power, mills and railways, from Britain in the 1700s.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="207" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Industrial Revolution</span><span class="kd-card-d">A great change to machines, steam power and factories that began in Britain in the 1700s.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/steam-engine.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Steam engine</span><span class="kd-card-d">Steam pushing a piston makes it work; by the 1800s such engines powered factories, railways and ships.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/james-watt"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-watt.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="401" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">James Watt</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Scottish inventor whose improved steam engines drove mills and pumped water out of mines.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/spinning-jenny"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spinning-jenny.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="264" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Spinning jenny</span><span class="kd-card-d">A spinning machine of 1764 to 1765 with which one worker could work eight or more spools at once.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/richard-arkwright"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-arkwright.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="397" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Richard Arkwright</span><span class="kd-card-d">Often called the father of the factory system, he built a water-powered cotton mill at Cromford.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/josiah-wedgwood"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josiah-wedgwood.jpg" alt="" width="330" height="433" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Josiah Wedgwood</span><span class="kd-card-d">A potter who led the change to making pottery in factories; his Etruria Works opened in 1769.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/bridgewater-canal"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bridgewater-canal.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="211" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Bridgewater Canal</span><span class="kd-card-d">Opened in 1761 to carry coal to Manchester, it halved the price of coal there.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool-and-manchester-railway"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liverpool-and-manchester-railway.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="259" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Liverpool and Manchester Railway</span><span class="kd-card-d">Opened in 1830, the first railway between two cities to run only on steam locomotives.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-faraday.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="423" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Michael Faraday</span><span class="kd-card-d">Largely self-taught, he discovered in 1831 the idea behind electric generators, and gave popular science lectures.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/robert-owen"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-owen.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="404" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Robert Owen</span><span class="kd-card-d">He ran the cotton mills of New Lanark in Scotland and opened a school there with free education in 1816.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/factory-acts"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/factory-acts.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="219" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Factory Acts</span><span class="kd-card-d">Laws from 1802 onwards; the Act of 1833 stopped textile mills employing children under nine.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/great-exhibition"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-exhibition.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="212" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Great Exhibition</span><span class="kd-card-d">A great show of inventions in London's Crystal Palace in 1851, visited by about six million people.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-industrial">Play the The Industrial Revolution Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-winter" aria-labelledby="t-winter-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-winter-h">Winter Festivals</h2><p class="kd-intro">Lights, feasts and songs for the darkest days of the year.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winter-solstice.jpg" alt="" width="330" height="220" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Winter solstice</span><span class="kd-card-d">The shortest day and longest night of the year. Old monuments such as Stonehenge line up with its sunrise or sunset.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/saturnalia"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saturnalia.jpg" alt="" width="330" height="331" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Saturnalia</span><span class="kd-card-d">A December festival of ancient Rome, with a public banquet, gift-giving and masters serving their slaves at table.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/yule"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yule.jpg" alt="" width="330" height="440" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Yule</span><span class="kd-card-d">A winter festival of the old Germanic peoples. Its name still means Christmas in English and other northern languages.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/dongzhi"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dongzhi.jpg" alt="" width="330" height="247" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Dongzhi</span><span class="kd-card-d">The Chinese winter solstice festival, when families gather to eat tangyuan, balls of sticky rice that stand for reunion.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/yalda-night"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yalda-night.jpg" alt="" width="330" height="236" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Yalda Night</span><span class="kd-card-d">On the longest night of the year, families in Iran and nearby lands stay up late to eat, read poetry and share pomegranates and watermelons.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hanukkah"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanukkah.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hanukkah</span><span class="kd-card-d">An eight-day Jewish festival, usually in December, when one more candle or lamp is lit each night.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/saint-lucys-day"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-lucys-day.jpg" alt="" width="330" height="261" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Saint Lucy's Day</span><span class="kd-card-d">A festival of light on 13 December. In the legend, Lucy wore a wreath of candles on her head to light her way.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/christmas"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christmas.jpg" alt="" width="330" height="272" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Christmas</span><span class="kd-card-d">The feast of the birth of Jesus, kept by most Christians on 25 December, with customs old and new.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-tree"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christmas-tree.jpg" alt="" width="330" height="518" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Christmas tree</span><span class="kd-card-d">The custom of a decorated evergreen grew up in Germany and Livonia. Records tell of a tree in Strasbourg's cathedral in 1539.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/santa-claus"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santa-claus.jpg" alt="" width="330" height="412" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Santa Claus</span><span class="kd-card-d">The gift-bringer of Christmas Eve grew out of the stories of Saint Nicholas. A poem of 1823 gave him a sleigh pulled by flying reindeer.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hogmanay"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hogmanay.jpg" alt="" width="330" height="258" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hogmanay</span><span class="kd-card-d">The New Year in Scotland. The first visitor after midnight brings gifts such as coal, shortbread and black bun.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/omisoka"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/omisoka.jpg" alt="" width="330" height="219" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ōmisoka</span><span class="kd-card-d">The last day of the year in Japan. At midnight most Buddhist temples strike a large bell 108 times.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-winter">Play the Winter Festivals Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section>
