tankhamun became pharaoh when he was only about nine years old.",""],["The Romans held a winter festival called Saturnalia, with feasts, games and presents.",""],["The Maya kept two calendars at once, one of 260 days and one of 365 days.",""],["Under Genghis Khan's sons and grandsons, the Mongol Empire became the largest land empire there has ever been.","/empire/mongol-empire"],["Marco Polo amazed Europeans by describing paper money, which he had seen in China.","/encyclopedia/marco-polo"],["The Eiffel Tower was built for a world's fair in 1889 and was only meant to stand for 20 years.",""],["Louis XIV of France reigned for 72 years, longer than any other European king.","/encyclopedia/louis-xiv"],["Isaac Newton also ran England's Royal Mint and hunted down people who made fake coins.","/encyclopedia/isaac-newton"],["Gutenberg printed his famous Bible in Mainz around 1455.","/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg"],["Hannibal crossed the Alps in 218 BCE with an army and about 37 elephants.","/encyclopedia/hannibal"],["The Silk Road was not one road but a web of trade routes stretching across Asia.","/encyclopedia/silk-road"],["Alexander the Great founded many cities called Alexandria; the most famous is in Egypt.","/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"],["The Tower of London once kept a royal zoo, with lions and even a polar bear.",""],["The ancient Egyptians loved cats, and many cats were mummified.",""],["In Roman public toilets, people sat side by side on long stone benches.",""],["China's first emperor, Qin Shi Huang, was buried with an army of thousands of life-size clay soldiers.","/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang"],["Actors in ancient Greek plays wore big masks so that everyone in the theatre could tell who they were playing.",""],["The Aztec capital, Tenochtitlan, was built on an island in the middle of a lake.","/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan"],["Cyrus the Great of Persia is remembered for letting the peoples he conquered keep their own customs.","/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great"],["The Library of Alexandria may have held hundreds of thousands of scrolls.","/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria"],["Medieval castles had toilets called garderobes, which often emptied straight into the moat.",""],["At the pyramid of Chichén Itzá in Mexico, the evening sun at the equinox makes a shadow like a snake.",""],["Cleopatra came from a Greek family that ruled Egypt for nearly 300 years.","/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii"],["The Colosseum in Rome could hold at least 50,000 people.",""],["Roman towns had snack bars that sold hot food to people in the street.",""],["The oldest photograph that still survives was taken in France around 1826.",""],["The Wright brothers' first powered flight, in 1903, lasted just 12 seconds.",""],["The windows around the base of the Hagia Sophia's dome make it look as if it is floating.","/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia"],["The Inca had no alphabet, but they kept records with knotted strings called quipu.","/empire/inca-empire"],["Inca messengers ran in relays along mountain roads to carry news across the empire.","/empire/inca-empire"],["Queen Victoria was the first ruler of Britain to live in Buckingham Palace.","/encyclopedia/queen-victoria"],["Peter the Great of Russia was more than two metres tall.","/encyclopedia/peter-the-great"],["The ancient Egyptians made one of the first calendars with 365 days.",""],["Kites were invented in China more than 2,000 years ago.",""],["Chess began in India about 1,500 years ago, in a game called chaturanga.",""],["The Rosetta Stone is a piece of a bigger stone: its top and one corner are missing.","/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone"],["Ancient Greek runners at Olympia raced in a stadium about 192 metres long.","/encyclopedia/ancient-olympic-games"],["The Statue of Liberty was a gift from the people of France to the United States in 1886.",""],["Sparta's kings were always two at a time, from two different royal families.","/encyclopedia/sparta"],["The Vikings reached North America about a thousand years ago, at L'Anse aux Meadows in Canada.","/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows"],["Zheng He's fleet in 1405 had more than 200 ships and about 27,000 sailors and soldiers.","/encyclopedia/zheng-he"],["Ibn Battuta's travels covered about 120,000 kilometres, three times around the Earth.","/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta"],["The word \"algorithm\" comes from the name of the Baghdad scholar al-Khwarizmi.","/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi"],["In 1610 Galileo published drawings of the Moon's mountains and craters, seen through his own telescope.","/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei"],["Machu Picchu's finest walls were built without mortar: their stones are cut to fit together tightly.","/encyclopedia/machu-picchu"],["The Taj Mahal took about 20 years and 20,000 workers to build.","/encyclopedia/taj-mahal"],["The Chinese invented paper, printing, the magnetic compass and gunpowder, often called the Four Great Inventions.","/encyclopedia/papermaking"],["The Inca built a network of roads about 40,000 kilometres long across the Andes.","/encyclopedia/qhapaq-nan"],["Polynesian voyagers reached Hawaii, New Zealand and Easter Island long before Europeans sailed the Pacific.","/encyclopedia/polynesians"],["The Indian mathematician Brahmagupta wrote rules for calculating with zero in the year 628.",""],["Samurai armour was made of small plates laced together with cords of silk or leather.","/encyclopedia/samurai"],["The people of Mesopotamia counted in sixties, which is why an hour has 60 minutes.","/encyclopedia/sumer"],["Great Zimbabwe gave its name to the modern country of Zimbabwe.","/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe"],["The Aztecs used cacao beans as money.","/encyclopedia/cacao"],["The Mongol army was organised in units of ten, a hundred, a thousand and ten thousand soldiers.","/encyclopedia/decimal-system-mongol-army"],["Mongol messengers changed to fresh horses at the stations of the Yam, so news crossed the empire quickly.","/encyclopedia/yam-postal-system"],["The words \"magic\" and \"magician\" come from the magi, the priests of ancient Iran.","/encyclopedia/magi"],["Persian royal riders, changing horses at stations, could cover the Royal Road in nine days; on foot it took ninety.","/encyclopedia/royal-road"],["The English phrase \"parting shot\" comes from the Parthian shot, when a horse archer turned in the saddle to shoot behind him.","/encyclopedia/parthian-shot"],["The oldest dated printed book that still survives is a copy of the Diamond Sutra from 868, found in a cave near Dunhuang.","/encyclopedia/diamond-sutra-of-dunhuang"],["Along the Silk Road travelled not only silk but spices, horses, religions and new technologies.","/encyclopedia/silk-road"],["Harald Hardrada, who later became a king, once served in the Byzantine emperor's Varangian Guard.","/encyclopedia/varangian-guard"],["In a siege, the Golden Horn, the harbour of Constantinople, could be closed with a great chain.","/encyclopedia/golden-horn"],["Greek fire could burn even on water, and what it was made of was a closely guarded secret.","/encyclopedia/greek-fire"],["Celtic languages are still spoken today in Ireland, Scotland, Wales and Brittany.","/encyclopedia/celts"],["The best-preserved broch, at Mousa in Shetland, still stands about 13 metres high.","/encyclopedia/broch"],["The Gundestrup cauldron is the largest known piece of European Iron Age silver work.","/encyclopedia/gundestrup-cauldron"],["Michelangelo chose much of his marble himself at the quarries of Carrara.","/encyclopedia/michelangelo"],["The paint of Leonardo's Last Supper began to flake within decades, because he tried a new way of painting on dry plaster.","/encyclopedia/the-last-supper-leonardo"],["Brunelleschi's dome on Florence Cathedral is still the largest masonry dome in the world.","/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi"],["The Ottoman Empire lasted more than six centuries, until the office of sultan was ended in 1922.","/encyclopedia/abolition-of-the-ottoman-sultanate"],["Mimar Sinan, the chief architect of the Ottoman Empire, designed hundreds of buildings.","/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan"],["Bursa, the first major capital of the Ottomans, was a centre of the silk trade.","/encyclopedia/bursa"],["The unit of power, the watt, is named after the Scottish inventor James Watt.","/encyclopedia/james-watt"],["Robert Owen's slogan was \"eight hours' labour, eight hours' recreation, eight hours' rest\".","/encyclopedia/robert-owen"],["The first recorded railway journey hauled by a steam locomotive was made in South Wales on 21 February 1804.","/encyclopedia/steam-locomotive"],["The Nutcracker, a ballet set on Christmas Eve, was first performed in Saint Petersburg on 18 December 1892.","/encyclopedia/the-nutcracker"],["On Christmas Eve 1968 the three astronauts of Apollo 8 were in orbit around the Moon.","/encyclopedia/apollo-8"],["Fairs were once held on the frozen River Thames in London. The last one began on 1 February 1814 and lasted four days.","/encyclopedia/river-thames-frost-fairs"]];</script>
